WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.540
George: There are no southern conservative
democrats anywhere, there are no liberal republicans

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:05.540
anymore.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.880
And is everybody happy?

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.990
I don't think so.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.579
Jonah: Hi, George.

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:19.029
Thanks for being here.

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:23.240
Delighted to talk to you about "The Conservative
Sensibility," which is just out.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:28.160
And you should know, listeners should know,
viewers should know that this will be repurposed

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.640
for my podcast, "The Remnant."

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.040
George: Remnant of what?

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.710
Jonah: Well, you remember Albert Jay Nock?

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:35.710
George: Of course.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:37.560
"Memoirs of the Superfluous Man."

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.640
Jonah: That's right and "My Enemy, the State."

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:46.620
And he wrote a wonderful essay for the "Atlantic
Magazine" in I believe 1936 called "Isaiah's

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.620
Job."

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:54.870
And it's one of the best essays in the history
of sort of conservatism, liberalism, libertarianism,

00:00:54.870 --> 00:01:01.550
in which he basically tells people, "Don't
worry about trying to persuade the masses.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:08.072
All you can do is speak to the remnant of
those who hold on to correct thinking," I

00:01:08.072 --> 00:01:13.400
think is, I wanna say, Walter Benjamin paraphrased.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.540
And I don't know if you've noticed, but when
things started to go a little wobbly among

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:23.010
the Republican Party and the Conservative
Movement, I returned to my love.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:28.610
Albert Jay Nock, I'm a huge fan of, and I
called the few of us left who haven't...hadn't

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.680
taken the crazy pills, the remnant.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.220
And it became the perfect name for my podcast.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.390
George: Very good.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:39.830
Jonah: And so, in some ways, you are the titular
deity of the remnant because you actually,

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:44.380
at a moment where almost everybody else is
zagging towards some version of nationalism

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:51.220
or collectivism or Catholic integralism, which
is the new hot thing, or as our friend, Matt

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:57.140
Continetti recently put it, post-liberal-conservatism,
you were zigging the other direction towards

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:02.310
actual classical liberalism and liberalism
rightly understood.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.820
So your book is called "The Conservative Sensibility."

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:12.510
And why don't you...I talked to you in some
length about your book on C-SPAN, we will

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.420
put a link up to that in the show notes.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.760
But why don't you, for the sake of listeners
who bizarrely didn't tune into that on a weekend,

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:22.909
sort of make your basic case.

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:25.010
I'm sure you haven't done that yet on the
book tour.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:30.790
George: Well, I have noticed and I think lots
of Americans have noticed that things aren't

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:31.790
going well.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:38.459
The prestige of government has fallen, as
the pretenses of government have risen.

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:43.600
I thought it was time for, among other things,
an exercise in intellectual archaeology to

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.480
excavate the foundations of the Republic.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:53.530
And I thought of this as a Princeton PhD in
terms of an argument between two Princetonians,

00:02:53.530 --> 00:03:01.439
James Madison of the great class of 1771 and
Tommy, as he was known at Princeton, Thomas

00:03:01.439 --> 00:03:06.219
Woodrow Wilson of the class of 1879.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:11.069
Wilson being the first president to criticize
the American founding, which meant criticizing

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:17.520
the constitutional architecture that Madison
erected in response to the founders' belief

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.870
in the natural rights which precede government.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:24.760
Wilson rejected this with remarkable forthrightness.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:32.239
And it turns out, with remarkable success,
as he and subsequent progressives have made

00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:39.189
hash of the separation of powers with the
predictable consequence of an emancipated

00:03:39.189 --> 00:03:40.189
president.

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:47.120
Jonah: So, long-term listeners of this podcast
know I consider myself one of the nation's

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:51.379
foremost despisers of Woodrow Wilson, so this
is an easy...

00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:52.859
George: Get in line.

00:03:52.859 --> 00:03:59.269
Jonah: As I said on the C-SPAN conversation,
in some ways, my only criticism of your criticism

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:00.889
of Wilson is it doesn't quite go far enough.

00:04:00.889 --> 00:04:06.780
You don't get deep into the political prisoners
or the attacking they hyphenated Americans

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.279
or the war on the German language.

00:04:10.279 --> 00:04:11.739
But it's a good start.

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:22.660
And part of your argument, which I share entirely,
is that what Wilson did was he represented

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:29.090
the intellectual zeitgist of the time which
was besotted with Bismarck and Darwin, right?

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:35.490
And so, part of his critique of the original...of
the founding, was that it was too Newtonian

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:39.310
and it needed to become Darwinian and adapt
to the times.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:44.490
Now, part of your argument, which again I
agree with, is that we've never quite gotten

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:45.490
over this notion.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.680
That's what the living Constitution basically
comes from.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.420
How are we ever gonna get rid of it?

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:51.450
George: Change.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.110
Change public opinion.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:59.470
At the end of the day, even Supreme Court
justices are nominated by elected people and

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:02.170
confirmed by elected people.

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:06.730
And so if you understand that public opinion
is shiftable sand, you get busy trying to

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:07.730
shift it.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:12.800
Jonah: I agree with that entirely, persuasion
is not the ideal tool, but it's the only we've

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:13.800
got.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:14.800
George: Exactly.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.290
Jonah: But part of your argument, which is
a little radical to some years, I believe

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:29.840
it's in chapter four, is persuading judges
to use their powers to not give a damn about

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.240
public opinion, and side on liberalism or
on liberty.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.220
Why don't you sort of explain that for a second?

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:41.960
George: Well, there comes a point at which
judicial deference to democratic legislatures

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:43.470
is dereliction of duty.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:48.270
The duty to superintend the excesses of democracy.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:54.820
For years, conservatives upset by the freewheeling
rights manufacturers of the Warren Court said,

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:58.220
"What we really need is more deference."

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:02.200
They were picking up the conservative line
that accelerated Oliver Wendell Holmes and

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:07.290
others who said, Holmes famously, "If the
people want to go to hell, I will help them.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:10.310
It's my job."

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:14.120
Meaning that the excesses of democracy are
what democracy's all about and he could care

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.150
less.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:24.070
I believe that there is a higher obligation
than to the majoritarian ethos of the times,

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:30.520
it is to the Constitution and the so-called
counter majoritarian difficulty, which was

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:35.990
a phrase put into our vocabulary by a great
mean, Alexander Bickel of [inaudible 00:06:35]

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:42.490
law school who was a tutor of, friend of,
colleague of Robert Bork.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:47.640
He said that, "The Supreme Court and Judicial
Review is an anomalous institution in a majoritarian

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.840
society and inherently problematic."

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.700
I just don't think that's true for several
reasons.

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:58.580
First, most of what modern government does
has nothing to do with majorities, and everything

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:06.920
to do with compact, small, intense, educated,
articulate, confident, and well-lawyer factions.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:14.110
And therefore, when the majoritarian institutions
responsive to these factions does something

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:20.660
peculiarly offensive to the rights of the
citizen, it's the court's job to swat it down.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.730
That's all.

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:28.570
The Constitution, after all, is a tapestry,
and particularly the Bill of Rights, of prohibitions

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:30.570
on what majorities can do.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.860
You want an established church, sorry, you
can't have it.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:38.260
You want restrictions on free exercise of
religion, can't have that either.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:43.070
Even if a majority wants unreasonable searches
and seizures, can't do it.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:45.720
Jonah: You mentioned you can't have an established
church.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.250
There were states that had established churches
around the time of [crosstalk 00:07:48].

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:49.250
George: Indeed.

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:50.650
Jonah: Do you think states could go back to
having established churches?

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:52.240
George: I do not.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:57.450
I'm what Scalia called himself a faint-hearted
originalist.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:03.420
But to me, originalism first breaks on the
Eighth Amendment.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:06.190
There should be no cruel and unusual punishments.

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:11.890
The cruel and unusual punishments that were
being inflicted in Philadelphia in the summer

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:16.860
of 1787 would make Scalia and me and everyone
else blanch.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:21.790
So what you try and figure is what they Eighth
Amendment tried to do, is put the country

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:24.790
on record against cruelty.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:30.640
And I'm sorry to say that Earl Warren got
it right, there are evolving standards of

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:35.289
decency that mark the evolution of a maturing
society.

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:37.139
And you have to take that into account.

00:08:37.139 --> 00:08:39.279
The same is true with religion.

00:08:39.279 --> 00:08:46.499
Jonah: A big part of your argument, I think
you state it forthrightly in the introduction,

00:08:46.499 --> 00:08:51.850
is that, I'm paraphrasing, the task of American
conservatism is to conserve the founding and

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:54.300
the principles that created it.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:09:01.240
I'm very sympathetic to that and I think as
a practical matter, the job of the conservative

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:06.699
goes to office to do conservative, packs his
lunch and all of the rest, that a big chunk

00:09:06.699 --> 00:09:09.500
of his job boils down to that.

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:15.829
But one of my sort of philosophical problems...this
is very much like a very much Claremont review

00:09:15.829 --> 00:09:21.240
of books position certainly prior to the current
occupant of the White House, which was that

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:26.800
the American founding is the fundament of
conservative fundamentalism.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.910
What's wrong with saying Western Civilization
is the fundament of...is the backdrop...

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:34.930
Here's my problem.

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:43.529
I'm against fundaments, reducing all of conservatism
as a philosophical matter to the founding

00:09:43.529 --> 00:09:51.430
leaves out all sorts of other strains of intellectual
thought, cultural norms that are also extremely

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:52.430
important.

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:55.720
Why say this is what conservatism is?

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:01.779
George: This is what American conservatism
is, and the adjective does a lot of work in

00:10:01.779 --> 00:10:04.050
modifying that noun.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:10.899
We're not blood and soil, throw on altar,
tribal conservatism in the European strain

00:10:10.899 --> 00:10:17.430
that has evolved to defend existing hierarchies
and orders and institutions.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:24.600
We're semi-Burkean in that Burke had a lively
appreciation of Hayek came to call the spontaneous

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:29.579
order of an evolving natural society.

00:10:29.579 --> 00:10:35.709
We go a step farther, however, in saying that
we want to reconcile people to the openness,

00:10:35.709 --> 00:10:39.059
the exhilaration of an open society.

00:10:39.059 --> 00:10:45.699
Obviously, the United States owes an enormous
debt to various strands of Western Civilization,

00:10:45.699 --> 00:10:49.310
particularly the English Enlightenment.

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:56.879
But we're better at it, that's why, America,
there's something sublime about the United

00:10:56.879 --> 00:11:01.240
States attempt to have a continental republic.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:08.120
Till the Americans came along and Madison
revolutionized democratic theory, there was

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:12.920
agreement among the few people who thought
democracy was possible that if possible, it

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:17.399
had to be in a small, face-to-face society.

00:11:17.399 --> 00:11:18.930
Madison said that's exactly wrong.

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:25.220
A small, face-to-face society is apt to be
homogenous and therefore, have a stable and

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:29.750
potentially tyrannical majority to the peril
of minorities.

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:36.529
"Therefore," he said, " have a continental,
an extensive republic in which government

00:11:36.529 --> 00:11:41.930
protects the different and unequal capacities
of acquiring property, which guarantees a

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:48.220
saving multiplicity of factions, which means
stable, shifting majorities that are not a

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:49.220
threat to minorities."

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:50.980
Jonah: An ecosystem, in a sense.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:55.139
George: Yes, he had a sociology of political
virtue, and that was it.

00:11:55.139 --> 00:12:00.300
Jonah: So you've been busy on book tour, so
you're probably not wading deep into the weeds

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:08.649
on the current obsessions of pointy-headed
conservatives of different types right this

00:12:08.649 --> 00:12:09.649
moment.

00:12:09.649 --> 00:12:14.160
And actually, you've always kind of stayed
somewhat isolated from a lot of this stuff,

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:15.399
which I wanna talk to you about in a little
bit.

00:12:15.399 --> 00:12:22.630
But for the last three weeks, there has emerged
what some of us are calling the French Wars.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:27.160
My very close friend and former colleague
at "National Review," David French, was the

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.970
target of what I would consider to be a generally
unfair attack by another friend of mine, we're

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:35.950
not nearly close, Sohrab Ahmari of the "New
York Post."

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:44.339
And it turns out that while no one was paying
attention, the Elbren Pozelle [SP] senior

00:12:44.339 --> 00:12:48.670
argument that we actually need to live in
basically a theocratic society where the state

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:57.389
imposes a notion of the higher good or the
highest good, has erupted out of nowhere.

00:12:57.389 --> 00:13:06.369
One of the reasons why I wanna bring this
up is that I think David is a truly unfair

00:13:06.369 --> 00:13:12.660
target of all of this ire and you are actually
a far more fitting one.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:17.779
But I think a lot of these people are afraid
to attack you and so they go after poor David.

00:13:17.779 --> 00:13:23.220
But David is a Evangelical Christian, he's
been fighting for Christian...you know, for

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:26.889
religious liberty and campus free speech stuff
for decades.

00:13:26.889 --> 00:13:33.910
And his great sin is that he refuses to say
that Donald Trump is a man of good character

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:35.050
and all of these kinds of things.

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:39.829
George: Well he's also accused, I gather,
of having good manners, he's polite.

00:13:39.829 --> 00:13:41.110
Oh my god.

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:48.529
Jonah: Yes, and there's this theory that was
is required is to in some way imbibe the pugnaciousness

00:13:48.529 --> 00:13:52.670
of Donald Trump and that will lead to victory
of some kind.

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:54.629
Have you weighted into any of this?

00:13:54.629 --> 00:14:00.740
George: No, I've watched from afar and I've
seen this eruption.

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:08.209
This is an outgrowth of the Flight 93 election
syndrome which is that the end is nigh unless

00:14:08.209 --> 00:14:11.260
people listen to people like us.

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:16.360
It's a way of pumping up the grandeur and
magnificence and importance of people who

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:22.040
say, "We stand at Armageddon, and things have
never been worse, but they might get worse

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:24.709
tomorrow unless radical things are done."

00:14:24.709 --> 00:14:28.529
Jonah: Right, and people who disagree with
you must shut up for we are at an existential

00:14:28.529 --> 00:14:29.529
crisis.

00:14:29.529 --> 00:14:35.350
George: Exactly, existential crisis, but the
self-dramatizing, things aren't that bad.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:38.430
I mean, I'm not happy.

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:42.899
No one writes political philosophy if they're
content, right?

00:14:42.899 --> 00:14:47.959
Because you're irritated about something or
anxious or afraid or something.

00:14:47.959 --> 00:14:51.740
But I just think this hysteria is to be ignored.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:55.100
Jonah: I quote you in one of my previous books.

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:59.860
There's a story you tell about how when you
first got your syndicated column, you called

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:05.839
George...William F. Buckley, "How the heck
am I going to write two columns a week?"

00:15:05.839 --> 00:15:06.839
What was his advice to you?

00:15:06.839 --> 00:15:08.800
George: He said, "The world irritates me three
times a week."

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:09.980
He wrote three times a week.

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:11.680
He said, "The world irritates me."

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:18.259
And it turns out it's true, the world irritates
or amuses or piques my curiosity 100 times

00:15:18.259 --> 00:15:19.759
a year.

00:15:19.759 --> 00:15:23.699
I've never had a day when I didn't have three
or four things I wanted to write about.

00:15:23.699 --> 00:15:24.699
Jonah: Yeah, you keep a list, right?

00:15:24.699 --> 00:15:25.699
You carry it around?

00:15:25.699 --> 00:15:27.269
George: I do, I've got it in my pocket.

00:15:27.269 --> 00:15:32.319
Jonah: So one of the things I think is sort
of fascinating about this moment is the conservative

00:15:32.319 --> 00:15:36.999
consensus, as the "First Things" crowd calls
is, they claim is shattered, which I think

00:15:36.999 --> 00:15:38.470
is a little bit of wish casting.

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:40.120
But maybe they're right.

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:42.009
George: What was it?

00:15:42.009 --> 00:15:45.160
Jonah: Well, basically it was fusionism.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:58.680
Frank Meyer's notion of balancing notions...fusionism
as shorthand is a free society can only be

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.929
virtuous if virtue is freely chosen.

00:16:00.929 --> 00:16:04.319
Virtue imposed is not virtuous.

00:16:04.319 --> 00:16:09.249
And you create people that will lead themselves
to tyranny if you think that you can impose

00:16:09.249 --> 00:16:11.179
virtue from above.

00:16:11.179 --> 00:16:18.189
And Tucker Carlson, a longtime friend of mine,
Yoram Hazony, Patrick Deneen, the guys at

00:16:18.189 --> 00:16:22.670
"First Things," Zohrab [SP], they've basically,
to one extent or another, it's difficult to

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:27.180
generalize, have turned their back primarily
on this notion of free markets.

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:33.980
And they claim, as Tucker Carlson put it,
that the Washington consensus is that it is

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:37.420
entirely libertarian, and that Washington
is run by libertarians.

00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:40.459
Which that is the sane response.

00:16:40.459 --> 00:16:41.459
George: Is he new?

00:16:41.459 --> 00:16:43.269
How long has he lived in this town?

00:16:43.269 --> 00:16:47.860
Jonah: Well, there's a shell game going because
what they do is they define sort of the Lockean

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:52.949
[SP] liberal order as essentially libertarian
because the individual autonomy of free choice

00:16:52.949 --> 00:16:58.420
is a libertarian notion, and so therefore
supporting free markets and whatnot, amounts

00:16:58.420 --> 00:16:59.759
to libertarianism.

00:16:59.759 --> 00:17:03.019
Which I think there are many stolen bases
in that journey there.

00:17:03.019 --> 00:17:05.449
George: And un-free markets amounts to what?

00:17:05.449 --> 00:17:07.809
Jonah: Immanentizing the eschaton, I don't
know.

00:17:07.809 --> 00:17:10.899
George: Elizabeth Warrenism, socialism, whatever
we call it.

00:17:10.899 --> 00:17:14.890
Jonah: So Tucker, the other night, came out
in lavish praise on Elizabeth Warren getting

00:17:14.890 --> 00:17:15.890
on the plane.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:19.610
George: He should have, because he is a working-class
socialist.

00:17:19.610 --> 00:17:21.029
He's not exactly working class.

00:17:21.029 --> 00:17:24.899
I don't know how many of them he rubs elbows
with at the Metropolitan Club.

00:17:24.899 --> 00:17:26.089
Jonah: But he likes them.

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:27.779
George: He likes them from a distance.

00:17:27.779 --> 00:17:29.460
Jonah: So you actually...

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:33.270
This the gossip portion of these things.

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:35.799
You started at "National Review" in '71?

00:17:35.799 --> 00:17:36.799
George: '73.

00:17:36.799 --> 00:17:37.799
Jonah: '73.

00:17:37.799 --> 00:17:39.740
George: As Frank Meyer was dying of cancer.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:41.380
Jonah: Did you know Frank Meyer at all?

00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:42.380
George: I did.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:47.940
I went up and saw him and his wife at their...they
were at Woodstock, as I recall, in upstate,

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:50.240
from the city in Hudson Valley.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.470
Jonah: I didn't realize he was telecommuting
them, but...

00:17:53.470 --> 00:17:54.570
George: Well, he wasn't.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:59.330
He was sick and Bill said, "You wanna be a
columnist?

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:00.970
Could you also do the back of the book?"

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:04.179
And I, knowing nothing about it, said, "Oh,
sure."

00:18:04.179 --> 00:18:05.820
And that's how I got to know Frank.

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:09.870
Jonah: So Frank was, for those who don't know,
was the literary editor and essentially the

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:14.169
managing editor at least for a time of the
magazine.

00:18:14.169 --> 00:18:15.169
What was he like?

00:18:15.169 --> 00:18:21.870
George: A man with earnestness with goodwill
and cheerfulness.

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:25.740
Jonah: What was it like working at "National
Review" back in those days?

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:31.840
George: Well, it was great in part because
of Priscilla Buckley, Bill's sister, who made

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:33.549
the trains run on time.

00:18:33.549 --> 00:18:34.590
She was good.

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:36.760
And Bill was fun.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.940
Bill is, again, cheerful.

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:40.340
Jonah: And polite.

00:18:40.340 --> 00:18:43.279
George: Impeccably polite, yes, Frenchism.

00:18:43.279 --> 00:18:44.279
Jonah: That's right.

00:18:44.279 --> 00:18:45.279
George: Premature Frenchism.

00:18:45.279 --> 00:18:46.940
Jonah: That's the thing.

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:50.280
When people ask me what it was like, I was
very lucky.

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:55.600
There's sort of a post-Buckley, pre-Buckley
who worked at "National Review" in my time,

00:18:55.600 --> 00:19:00.760
and I was fortunate enough to get to know
Bill and when I first...and I've been saying

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:03.370
this for years.

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:08.010
The first time I was invited to his Maisonette,
whatever you call that, on 73rd Street, I

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:13.149
was terrified because I thought it was gonna
be Chester in quotes and Latin puns all night

00:19:13.149 --> 00:19:14.880
long.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:21.210
And it turns out that Bill was...if the definition
of good manners isn't necessarily what fork

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:25.970
to use but making people feel respected and
listened to, he's arguably the best mannered

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:28.560
person I'd ever met in my life.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:31.409
And so it was interesting.

00:19:31.409 --> 00:19:38.510
I was reading this book about Bill Rusher,
who's the longtime publisher at "National

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:43.610
Review," and the author was making this point
that one of the great advantages of William

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:50.990
F. Buckley was that at a time when the media
was so determined to make George Wallace seem

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:57.269
like the face of what the right was, Buckley
comes along, mannered erudite, can out debate

00:19:57.269 --> 00:19:59.950
Galbraith and all these kinds of people.

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:04.990
And even though you were annoying a good number
of people at "National Review" for calling

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:11.270
for Spiro Agnew to be dumped from the ticket
and all your Watergate columns, at the end

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:17.460
of the day, you were considered a net asset
because you were one of the only younger people

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:20.340
out there who could do the similar stuff that
Buckley was doing.

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:23.269
George: Yeah, it's a close call, but a net
asset.

00:20:23.269 --> 00:20:26.419
Jonah: But you did not win a lot of friends
on the right back in those days.

00:20:26.419 --> 00:20:29.590
You were a harsh critic of the Nixon administration.

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:31.230
George: Which didn't help me.

00:20:31.230 --> 00:20:38.070
I left the Senate staff at the end of 1972
to start a column with "National Review,"

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:42.559
and Meg Greenfield, the then Deputy Editor
of the "Washington Post" editorial page, said,

00:20:42.559 --> 00:20:45.230
"Submit the columns to us also."

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:49.850
So I did and they decided to start a syndication,
the "Washington Post Writers Group" in order

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:53.780
to syndicate David Broder, but it's as easy
to syndicate two as one.

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:56.050
So they said, "We'll do Will also."

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:58.799
So they said, "We have this great marketing
idea."

00:20:58.799 --> 00:21:04.299
Agnew had been crashing around the country,
mow-mowing the editorial page saying there's

00:21:04.299 --> 00:21:08.870
not enough conservative writers, nattering
nabobs of negativism and all the rest.

00:21:08.870 --> 00:21:13.331
And "The Post" said, "Fine, we'll tell the
editors, here's someone will at least support

00:21:13.331 --> 00:21:15.350
Nixon."

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:21.460
So I become a columnist in January '73 at
about the time Judge Sirica imposes the severe

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:26.750
penalties, probably excessive penalties, on
James McCord and others and the Watergate

00:21:26.750 --> 00:21:29.919
thing begins to unravel.

00:21:29.919 --> 00:21:36.720
So, I called it as I saw it and I thought
where there's a lot of smoke, there's a lot

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:39.309
of smoke and I kept at this.

00:21:39.309 --> 00:21:49.480
And at one point, Nixon's Chief of Staff,
Alexander Haig, called me at my home and said,

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:51.700
"The boss really likes your writing.

00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:53.990
Maybe you'd like to come over and get on board
at the White House."

00:21:53.990 --> 00:22:00.890
And I said, "I am extremely flattered, but
you should read my next column before you

00:22:00.890 --> 00:22:03.809
extend this offer," and I never heard from
him again.

00:22:03.809 --> 00:22:10.480
Jonah: So over your long tenure as a columnist
and as a conservative and sort of one of the

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:19.679
Mount Rushmore figureheads of I'd say post-'65
conservatism, you've managed to do two things.

00:22:19.679 --> 00:22:26.639
And one of the things is obvious to a lot
of people, which is you managed to not go

00:22:26.639 --> 00:22:33.030
native and sort of mellow.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:35.289
Or, I don't know how to put this.

00:22:35.289 --> 00:22:36.289
Mellow is the wrong word.

00:22:36.289 --> 00:22:38.780
You didn't grow as Linda Greenhouse would
put it as you...

00:22:38.780 --> 00:22:39.780
George: Strange new respect.

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:40.780
Jonah: That's right.

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:46.440
As your profile increased, you still...I remember
a long time ago, this was in the '90s, I was

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:53.820
here at AI and you wrote a scathing column
about Dan Rostenkowski.

00:22:53.820 --> 00:23:03.870
And I remember Tom Mann being just stunned
that someone in your elite position could

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:08.460
remain as distant from the sort of swamp stuff.

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:11.650
The other part, which I don't think a lot
of people understand, is that you also kinda

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:17.630
kept at an arm's length a lot of the sort
of conservative industrial complex stuff as

00:23:17.630 --> 00:23:18.630
well.

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:20.120
I mean you had your start briefly...

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:24.030
Not start but you had your time at "National
Review," but you were in some way sort of

00:23:24.030 --> 00:23:25.350
like the "Reader's Digest," because...

00:23:25.350 --> 00:23:27.830
I mean this sincerely.

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:30.000
Conservative but apart.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.850
I don't mean silly, I mean...

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:34.029
George: Partly it's temperamental.

00:23:34.029 --> 00:23:35.720
Jonah: You're not a joiner.

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:40.850
George: Samuel Johnson described himself as
un-clubbable and I may be un-clubbable.

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:48.000
Jonah: So going back to the days of the Nixon
stuff, were the arguments then on the right

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:52.590
such as you were part of them, uglier or meaningfully
different than the arguments we're having

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:53.590
today about Trump?

00:23:53.590 --> 00:24:00.340
George: I think they were more civil and they
were more substantive and they weren't as

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:02.110
angry.

00:24:02.110 --> 00:24:05.850
Basically, conservatives knew what they were
angry about.

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:11.230
First of all, the Soviet Union, which we tend
to forget has gone away and organized a lot

00:24:11.230 --> 00:24:12.380
of thinking.

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:15.510
Arms control and all the rest.

00:24:15.510 --> 00:24:19.419
And then there was the question of pandemic
spending.

00:24:19.419 --> 00:24:24.120
This was at a time when it was still possible
to believe that Americans, conservatives in

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:28.679
public office cared about pandemic deficits.

00:24:28.679 --> 00:24:32.260
We now know they don't anymore, which simplifies
the argument somewhat.

00:24:32.260 --> 00:24:34.870
So it was different.

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:39.309
Jonah: If you had to diagnose the maladies
that explain that difference, where would

00:24:39.309 --> 00:24:43.639
you...how would you do the triage of your
diagnosis there?

00:24:43.639 --> 00:24:51.390
George: Well, many conservatives have succumb
to the temptation of power, Scripture tells

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:55.780
us, "Put not our faith in princes," they put
their faith in a prince.

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:59.299
And when he's gone, they'll pick another prince.

00:24:59.299 --> 00:25:05.590
They have fully absorbed the ethos of presidential
government, presidential society really.

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:13.720
I mean we have...no president has ever come
to close absorbing public attention and the

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:16.430
national consciousness, if you will, to this
one.

00:25:16.430 --> 00:25:26.000
Roosevelt, who sort of revolutionized presidential
rhetoric with using that marvelous new invention

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:30.769
radio, never came close, never tried to come
close.

00:25:30.769 --> 00:25:33.649
How many fireside chats would he do in a year?

00:25:33.649 --> 00:25:35.419
I don't even know.

00:25:35.419 --> 00:25:36.419
Maybe seven?

00:25:36.419 --> 00:25:41.990
That's a pre 5:00 a.m. tweet storm by the
current person.

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:47.710
So conservatives have happily been swept up
in the maelstrom of presidential-itis.

00:25:47.710 --> 00:25:56.289
Jonah: But isn't part of that a symptom of
the Madisonian sociology breaking down?

00:25:56.289 --> 00:25:57.289
George: Yes.

00:25:57.289 --> 00:26:01.660
Jonah: If you had healthy institutions where
people had roots there, they might not look

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:06.960
to Washington for a president to entertain
them or to give them meaning, whether it's

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.440
Barack Obama or Donald Trump.

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:13.029
George: Yes, and if members of Congress did
not think of themselves as team players, either

00:26:13.029 --> 00:26:18.030
on the president's team or on the visiting
team playing the president's team, you would

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:24.240
have something like the Madisonian institution
equilibrium, but that's gone.

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:27.170
Jonah: So doesn't that raise the prospect
then?

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:31.600
As much as I agree with you that persuasion
is very much important, that a huge amount

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:37.830
of our problems lie way upstream of Washington,
which I understand that some of this is in

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:43.179
your book, that none of the problems that
we see today, whether or not you think Donald

00:26:43.179 --> 00:26:47.710
Trump is a problem, or Barack Obama is a problem,
or the life of Julia Ethos [SP] is a problem,

00:26:47.710 --> 00:26:51.909
if you had healthier families and healthier
communities and healthier institutions like

00:26:51.909 --> 00:26:56.840
organized religion and whatnot, people would
not be looking to Washington to find meaning

00:26:56.840 --> 00:26:57.980
in their lives in the first place?

00:26:57.980 --> 00:26:58.990
George: I think that's right.

00:26:58.990 --> 00:27:05.060
And the search for meaning and collective
action in politics is the source of...ruined

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:12.420
the 20th Century, communism, fascism, all
the rest, the mass movements need a mass and

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:15.470
it needs the massification of people.

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:19.679
And what's scary is when people embrace that
consciously and enthusiastically.

00:27:19.679 --> 00:27:23.769
Jonah: There's that essay from Wilson about
leaders of men, where you bind up.

00:27:23.769 --> 00:27:24.769
George: Exactly.

00:27:24.769 --> 00:27:29.150
Well, in one of his I quoted, it's an epigraph
of one of my chapters, he says, "In the good

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:32.789
society, people will be like bees in a hive."

00:27:32.789 --> 00:27:34.190
Very strange aspiration.

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:36.880
But it was his.

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:39.590
People have to calm down about politics.

00:27:39.590 --> 00:27:44.770
Government is important, free markets are
government creations, I got that.

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:48.590
Government has lots to do, fill the potholes,
deliver the mail.

00:27:48.590 --> 00:27:52.860
I guess it doesn't do that so much anymore,
but do whatever it does.

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:55.110
That's plenty.

00:27:55.110 --> 00:27:57.429
Delivering meaning isn't one of them.

00:27:57.429 --> 00:28:06.100
Jonah: So this brings me back to the question
I asked earlier about state established churches.

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:11.220
As I get older, I become at the federal level,
more radically libertarian than I used to

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:19.200
be and at the local level, I get vastly more
communitarian than I used to be.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:25.470
What is so wrong with, first of all, bringing
back the Ninth, Tenth Amendment and all that,

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:32.190
that would be nice, but sending as much power
down the most local level possible?

00:28:32.190 --> 00:28:34.440
George: Subsidiarity, as the Catholics say.

00:28:34.440 --> 00:28:35.440
Jonah: Right.

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:36.440
George: Yes.

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:37.440
Jonah: And that would...

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:40.059
But so here's the question.

00:28:40.059 --> 00:28:44.289
Isn't part of the problem we have, it's not
so much the tyranny of majorities, it's tyranny

00:28:44.289 --> 00:28:45.850
of national majorities?

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:50.870
I'm much less concerned about the "tyranny
of majorities" at a local level.

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:55.330
George: That's exactly where Madison worried
about the tyrannies, which is why he wanted

00:28:55.330 --> 00:28:56.700
the extensive republic.

00:28:56.700 --> 00:29:03.789
He said, "In a local, small area where the
anti-federalists are celebrated, you are much

00:29:03.789 --> 00:29:06.460
more apt to have stable tyrannical majorities."

00:29:06.460 --> 00:29:07.460
We did indeed.

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:09.139
We had localism in Jim Crow law.

00:29:09.139 --> 00:29:10.630
Jonah: Sure, no, I agree with you.

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:16.780
Jonah: They reflected the majority opinion
in Greenville, Mississippi and in Gadsden,

00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:17.780
Alabama.

00:29:17.780 --> 00:29:25.690
And it took a national decision not to respect
local majorities, and it was a virtuous decision.

00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:27.360
Jonah: I agree with that entirely.

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:30.909
They were democratic tyrannies and they needed
to be smashed.

00:29:30.909 --> 00:29:39.450
But they also...the issue of slavery rises
to a first order question about natural rights,

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:44.370
the dignity of the individual and all these
kinds of things.

00:29:44.370 --> 00:29:53.000
Banning rap music or having curfews or a prohibition
on alcohol in some small town, it seems to

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:57.159
me that's an area where the federal government
should be entirely silent.

00:29:57.159 --> 00:30:00.400
George: Other than to say, "You can't do it
at the local level."

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:01.690
That's where you and I differ here.

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:02.690
Jonah: Okay.

00:30:02.690 --> 00:30:05.440
George: I'm not a prohibitionist or an anti-prohibitionist.

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:11.789
I do remember getting a jolt toward libertarianism
when I was driving with one of my children

00:30:11.789 --> 00:30:15.770
from Washington to Kiawah Island, South Carolina
where I have a home.

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:21.340
And I stopped for the night in Florence, South
Carolina on Sunday night, looking for my evening

00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:26.409
martini, and found that the local ayatollahs
had sad that was not going to happen.

00:30:26.409 --> 00:30:33.350
And I said, "Insufferable," and got more libertarian
as a result of that.

00:30:33.350 --> 00:30:39.519
But banning rap music, on what grounds?

00:30:39.519 --> 00:30:41.490
Exercising what power of government?

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:45.590
What to purify the culture, to sensor offensive
speech?

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:47.019
I don't wanna go down that.

00:30:47.019 --> 00:30:48.019
Jonah: Yeah, no, that's fair.

00:30:48.019 --> 00:30:52.500
I should say I'm not in favor of banning any
music or any that kind of stuff.

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:56.669
And in fact, my positions on the local level
would be fairly libertarian about many of

00:30:56.669 --> 00:30:57.669
these things.

00:30:57.669 --> 00:31:03.629
But being able to heavily regulate what you
can do in the public square in a local area,

00:31:03.629 --> 00:31:09.809
seems to me...here's part of my diagnosis
of the problem, is that when you send all

00:31:09.809 --> 00:31:17.059
this power to Washington or the federal regime
or whatever you wanna call it, it is inevitable

00:31:17.059 --> 00:31:22.590
that people at a local level, far away, are
gonna feel like unseen forces, whether you

00:31:22.590 --> 00:31:28.240
call them the deep state or the administrative
state or the globalists or whatever you wanna

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:30.529
call them, that they feel powerless.

00:31:30.529 --> 00:31:37.039
And that powerlessness is the classic kindling
for populist movements.

00:31:37.039 --> 00:31:42.139
And if you send power back down to the most
local level possible, the powers that be suddenly

00:31:42.139 --> 00:31:46.930
have names and you know who to fire, and you
know how to hold them accountable.

00:31:46.930 --> 00:31:51.009
And that also means that if they have that
power, they're gonna exercise it ways...I

00:31:51.009 --> 00:31:55.970
mean laboratories of democracy was a loaded
phrase, Brandeis had an agenda there.

00:31:55.970 --> 00:32:00.230
But letting people having the transcendental
imagination, which is a phrase you've used

00:32:00.230 --> 00:32:05.809
in a few columns, to make this a more interesting
country to drive across, where communities...

00:32:05.809 --> 00:32:10.590
Because as Antonin Scalia was the first to
point out to me, federalism was the best system

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:14.269
ever conceived of for maximizing human happiness,
because it lets the most people live the way

00:32:14.269 --> 00:32:16.090
they wanna live.

00:32:16.090 --> 00:32:17.090
And that...

00:32:17.090 --> 00:32:18.090
George: And they can vote with their feet.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:19.090
Jonah: That's right.

00:32:19.090 --> 00:32:20.090
George: If they don't like California...

00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:22.779
Jonah: If you can't get a martini in...not
Kiawah, wherever this other place was, you

00:32:22.779 --> 00:32:23.870
don't live there.

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:27.549
Or you do what Bill Buckley did and always
travel with your own alcohol.

00:32:27.549 --> 00:32:30.070
George: I learned my lesson.

00:32:30.070 --> 00:32:32.669
No, I agree with that.

00:32:32.669 --> 00:32:37.389
If power is to be exercised, it ought to be
exercised as much as possible at the local

00:32:37.389 --> 00:32:38.389
level.

00:32:38.389 --> 00:32:41.460
The question is how much power should be exercised
collectively through government.

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:42.500
Jonah: Okay.

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:43.539
That's fair.

00:32:43.539 --> 00:32:47.130
And that's a prudential question, I agree
with that.

00:32:47.130 --> 00:32:51.900
But when I talk to college kids about this,
you know, you have to open up by saying, "Slavery

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:53.450
was bad, they were right to crush that.

00:32:53.450 --> 00:32:54.700
Jim Crow was bad."

00:32:54.700 --> 00:33:01.159
I also like to point out, though, that Jim
Crow, it wasn't capitalism, it was stateism,

00:33:01.159 --> 00:33:06.759
it was the kind of public choice factions
that you despise conspiring against the public

00:33:06.759 --> 00:33:11.690
good to rig the system to constrain the local
labor markets so black people couldn't move,

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:12.690
right?

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:16.820
And so once they can see or explain all that,
I then say, "Now, do you really think that

00:33:16.820 --> 00:33:20.899
your local microbrewery needs the okay from
someone in Washington?

00:33:20.899 --> 00:33:27.429
Do you the Oregon State Health officials are
okay with this microbrewery poisoning you?

00:33:27.429 --> 00:33:33.330
Or why can't you get unpasteurized cheese
that tastes like death without Washington's

00:33:33.330 --> 00:33:34.350
approval?"

00:33:34.350 --> 00:33:36.380
And that part they kinda get.

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:41.850
Because young people today, hate the homogenization
of America that they think comes from capitalism.

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:45.970
Some of it comes from capitalism, but some
of it comes from an over-weaning federal government

00:33:45.970 --> 00:33:48.740
that runs everything, right?

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:53.000
Anyway, that's my...I do this often on this
podcast is rant about this.

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:54.000
So I apologize.

00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:55.580
George: That's all right.

00:33:55.580 --> 00:34:00.379
Jonah: You are not, in fact, pessimistic about
the future or about America?

00:34:00.379 --> 00:34:05.780
George: Well my book is a summons to intelligent
pessimism, not fatalism.

00:34:05.780 --> 00:34:11.710
Pessimism in the sense that there are so many
ways things can go wrong, and so many ways

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:18.069
that things have gone wrong in the human story
that it is wise to be alert to the looming

00:34:18.069 --> 00:34:21.399
possibility that it'll happen again.

00:34:21.399 --> 00:34:26.220
And we know what the problems of democracy
can be, we know some of them, and there are

00:34:26.220 --> 00:34:30.580
unimagined ones that are lurking around the
bend, so be alert, that's all.

00:34:30.580 --> 00:34:31.800
Be wary.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:33.491
It's like living in the city.

00:34:33.491 --> 00:34:38.559
There are wonderful pleasures to living in
the city, but there also requires a certain

00:34:38.559 --> 00:34:43.770
urban wariness about certain neighborhoods
and certain behaviors and you learn to live

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:44.770
that way.

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:45.770
It's true of democracy.

00:34:45.770 --> 00:34:49.550
Jonah: This is a fantastic segue to the story
I wanna tell, which I don't think you know

00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:50.550
of.

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:57.540
So the first time we met, I was an earnest
college student at a place called Goucher

00:34:57.540 --> 00:35:02.609
College, and I was part of coeducation, so
I'm...

00:35:02.609 --> 00:35:05.339
George: This is up in Baltimore.

00:35:05.339 --> 00:35:07.160
Jonah: Up in Baltimore.

00:35:07.160 --> 00:35:12.490
I'm not quite the Rosa Parks of gender integration,
but I did my bit.

00:35:12.490 --> 00:35:18.140
And you came to give a speech, and I was invited
to the president's house for a pre-meeting

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:21.030
among student leaders and faculty and whatnot.

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:26.690
And we were talking, and I begrudge you not
at all for not remembering this, and now having

00:35:26.690 --> 00:35:30.349
done so many campus visits, there are countless
people who come up to say, "You don't remember

00:35:30.349 --> 00:35:32.569
meeting me," and they're right.

00:35:32.569 --> 00:35:35.770
And this was also 30-something years ago.

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:43.290
And we were talking, and I explained to you
that Victor Lasky was my brother's godfather

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:44.860
and one of my father's best friends.

00:35:44.860 --> 00:35:49.510
And for the listeners who don't know, Victor
Lasky was a muck-raking right winger of a

00:35:49.510 --> 00:35:52.829
sort, but an interesting fellow.

00:35:52.829 --> 00:35:54.270
And my parents knew William Safire.

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:58.250
I don't think I told you that the first time
I met Pat Buchanan was at my bris, which was

00:35:58.250 --> 00:35:59.970
an interesting way to meet Pat Buchanan.

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:03.540
And as my friend, Rob Long, says, "That might
explain some of his attitudes towards the

00:36:03.540 --> 00:36:05.460
Jews."

00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:11.940
But it was all going great, and then at some
point, we were talking about New York City,

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:12.960
where I'm from.

00:36:12.960 --> 00:36:18.210
And this was probably at the height of the
crime wave, certainly of the murder wave.

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:24.980
And I mentioned that there times in high school
where my friends and I would walk through

00:36:24.980 --> 00:36:26.650
Central Park at night.

00:36:26.650 --> 00:36:32.180
And a look of disdain came over your face,
and you said something along lines of, "Well

00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:33.180
then you're an idiot."

00:36:33.180 --> 00:36:34.960
And the conversation sort of ended there.

00:36:34.960 --> 00:36:35.960
George: I see.

00:36:35.960 --> 00:36:37.900
Jonah: And that was fine, it was over and
you were actually correct.

00:36:37.900 --> 00:36:38.900
That's fine.

00:36:38.900 --> 00:36:40.280
I'm not...again, I don't begrudge.

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:47.520
And so, fast forward about five years, you
were in your term limits phase when you were...

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:49.020
Are you still on term limits?

00:36:49.020 --> 00:36:50.020
George: Yes.

00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:51.680
Beto [SP] and I are.

00:36:51.680 --> 00:36:53.020
Beto's come out for term limits.

00:36:53.020 --> 00:36:55.410
Jonah: And we'll come back to that in a second.

00:36:55.410 --> 00:37:00.320
I was a young policy gnome at the American
Enterprise Institute, very earnest.

00:37:00.320 --> 00:37:04.740
And you were giving your Bradley lecture about
term limits.

00:37:04.740 --> 00:37:08.780
And during the Q&amp;A, I said, "Wouldn't a lot
of the problems that you're addressing be

00:37:08.780 --> 00:37:12.569
solved if we just went back to the founders'
vision about how big congressional districts

00:37:12.569 --> 00:37:19.109
should be and that we should have a Congress
of about 5,000 or 6,000 people?"

00:37:19.109 --> 00:37:26.710
And the sovereign contempt with which you
greeted this question enraged me, and people

00:37:26.710 --> 00:37:27.880
laughed at me.

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:29.520
You won this exchange.

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:38.440
And I heed myself to my personal monastery
of my apartment on 16th Street, and pounded

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:43.950
the typewriter like Snoopy, coming up with
this irrefutable argument about all of this.

00:37:43.950 --> 00:37:48.710
And I worked on this and I worked on this,
and trimmed it down, and worked on it some

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:49.710
more.

00:37:49.710 --> 00:37:53.420
And I ended up, as happenstance would have
it, it was the first op-ed I ever submitted

00:37:53.420 --> 00:37:57.800
for publication, I sent to the "Wall Street
Journal," and they accepted it.

00:37:57.800 --> 00:38:04.740
And they sat on it for a few months, and then
the day after the election in 1992, I think

00:38:04.740 --> 00:38:07.930
it was...yeah, it was.

00:38:07.930 --> 00:38:12.800
It was Newt Gingrich, Bill Bennett and Jonah
Goldberg, and I was making the argument for

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:15.240
expanding the size of the House of Representatives.

00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:16.859
And I did it in a fun, mirthful kind of way.

00:38:16.859 --> 00:38:23.740
But when you talk about how annoyance is your
muse, if you had not humiliated me so, I am

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:26.170
not sure I ever would have gotten this book.

00:38:26.170 --> 00:38:27.210
So I owe it all to you.

00:38:27.210 --> 00:38:29.359
George: That's why I did it.

00:38:29.359 --> 00:38:33.819
As Franklin Roosevelt used to say after some
fortuity had rescued him, he say, "I planned

00:38:33.819 --> 00:38:35.089
it that way."

00:38:35.089 --> 00:38:37.069
Jonah: But I thought you should know this.

00:38:37.069 --> 00:38:39.349
George: I'm glad to know it.

00:38:39.349 --> 00:38:40.349
Jonah: Yeah.

00:38:40.349 --> 00:38:41.360
Getting back to term limits.

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:48.420
Would you say the problem of...if you have
term limits, all you're doing is handing power

00:38:48.420 --> 00:38:52.350
over to the sort of prominent bureaucracy
of Washington even more?

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:56.750
George: They're not that much more for it
to get, they've already done this without

00:38:56.750 --> 00:38:58.710
term limits.

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:00.970
It is a real danger.

00:39:00.970 --> 00:39:03.079
Every reform has problems.

00:39:03.079 --> 00:39:04.700
The question is, what's worse?

00:39:04.700 --> 00:39:11.060
And I have come to the conclusion that came
in...I wrote in '94, I think, called "Restoration:

00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:15.250
Congress, Term Limits, and the Recovery of
Deliberate Democracy."

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:19.800
I understand the problem of the permanent
government, I understand the problem of the

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:26.190
lobbyist community, I understand the problem
of draining institutional memory from the

00:39:26.190 --> 00:39:28.220
Senate and the House.

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:29.220
Got it.

00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:34.860
I just think that there's no safety in politics.

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:39.829
And you're gonna cause problems with terms
limits I just think on balance, fewer and

00:39:39.829 --> 00:39:41.770
more tractable problems.

00:39:41.770 --> 00:39:46.550
Jonah: One of the main drivers of the problem
with our politics is that despite the fact

00:39:46.550 --> 00:39:51.800
we live in one of the most partisans times
in history, our parties have never been weaker.

00:39:51.800 --> 00:39:56.349
And couldn't some of the problems that you
identify, and again, I think you identify

00:39:56.349 --> 00:40:01.450
correctly, be solved if we actually let the
parties be stronger and having an institutional

00:40:01.450 --> 00:40:05.109
interest in protecting their own brand and
having a time horizon beyond the next election?

00:40:05.109 --> 00:40:06.109
George: Yes.

00:40:06.109 --> 00:40:12.890
And one way to do that is to abolish all campaign
finance laws, all of which I think are constitutionally

00:40:12.890 --> 00:40:15.080
problematic at best.

00:40:15.080 --> 00:40:17.490
Let the money pour back in.

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:23.920
Reduce the role of candidates as free booting
entrepreneurs raising their own capital to

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:25.190
invest in themselves.

00:40:25.190 --> 00:40:29.309
Let the parties distribute money the way they
used to, very good idea.

00:40:29.309 --> 00:40:31.119
Jonah: And another would be, I would argue...

00:40:31.119 --> 00:40:33.260
Because I agree with you entirely.

00:40:33.260 --> 00:40:36.710
When Mitch McConnell said, "We're not getting
money out of politics, we're just getting

00:40:36.710 --> 00:40:44.470
the parties out of politics," and I think
he was proving right, but would another part

00:40:44.470 --> 00:40:47.869
of the solution be to have the parties give
up on the primary system?

00:40:47.869 --> 00:40:50.970
George: Back to the smoke-filled room in the
Blackstone Hotel in Chicago?

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:52.020
Jonah: Pretty much.

00:40:52.020 --> 00:40:55.900
George: Yeah, but who's gonna play Mayor Daly
and who's gonna play Boss Haig and who's gonna

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:57.940
play Boss Crump [SP] and all those people?

00:40:57.940 --> 00:41:00.200
Jonah: You could just have a mixed regime.

00:41:00.200 --> 00:41:03.770
Look, Elaine Kamarck makes this point, that
America is the only advanced industrialized

00:41:03.770 --> 00:41:07.970
democracy where the parties have completely
advocated the power to pick their own nominees.

00:41:07.970 --> 00:41:08.970
And...

00:41:08.970 --> 00:41:10.559
George: Yeah, which is the most important
thing they do.

00:41:10.559 --> 00:41:13.050
Jonah: It is what they do.

00:41:13.050 --> 00:41:15.230
And I think that has been disastrous.

00:41:15.230 --> 00:41:21.020
And Yuval Levin makes this point often, that
there are...one of the signature problems

00:41:21.020 --> 00:41:27.500
with our culture is that properly understood,
what an institution does is it forges character.

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:31.500
It forges you to give up your sort of your
Rousseau and the Noble Savage and bend yourself

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:35.210
to the needs of the institution, Marines being
the best example of that.

00:41:35.210 --> 00:41:40.010
And Yuval identifies the problem in our culture
is that we no longer see, or too many people

00:41:40.010 --> 00:41:45.420
no longer see institutions as something that
they surrender to, they see them as platforms

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:46.640
to perform on.

00:41:46.640 --> 00:41:51.849
And if you look at the story of Donald Trump
and Bernie Sanders, they did not see the Republican

00:41:51.849 --> 00:41:56.760
Party as an institution they should bend to,
they saw it as something at best to perform

00:41:56.760 --> 00:42:04.000
on, if not, to entertain by gladiatorially
disemboweling for public consumption.

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:09.680
And so at the very least, you could bring
back the notion of a robust system of delegates

00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:12.430
that are actually left up to state parties,
or something like that.

00:42:12.430 --> 00:42:18.420
George: Yeah, because of the democrats have
just completed the last, to use...coin the

00:42:18.420 --> 00:42:23.390
phrase the remnant of the old way of doing
things by further reducing...

00:42:23.390 --> 00:42:27.040
I don't know if they've entirely extinguished
the super delegates who are supposed to be

00:42:27.040 --> 00:42:28.720
the adults in the room.

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:30.740
They said, "It's our business, we are elected
officials.

00:42:30.740 --> 00:42:32.930
It's our business to win elections.

00:42:32.930 --> 00:42:34.240
Here's how you do it."

00:42:34.240 --> 00:42:36.940
But that's a lost argument.

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:41.940
Jonah: Yeah, but as you say, our task at hand
is persuasion, right?

00:42:41.940 --> 00:42:42.960
George: Right.

00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:48.260
Jonah: And it seems to me that I would have
an easier persuading party hacks that they

00:42:48.260 --> 00:42:55.710
should have more power than I should be able
to persuade the masses that they should pick

00:42:55.710 --> 00:42:59.010
up their own damn crap, or whatever.

00:42:59.010 --> 00:43:04.430
And so I think the reason an elite play here
where you can actually persuade institutions

00:43:04.430 --> 00:43:09.460
that they deserve to have more power, and
that might be an easier job than the rest.

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:13.520
Because what we're heading into is a system
that was not designed to be a parliamentary

00:43:13.520 --> 00:43:15.589
system behaving like a parliamentary system.

00:43:15.589 --> 00:43:16.589
George: Yeah.

00:43:16.589 --> 00:43:21.920
Well 60 years ago, the political scientists
in America said, "If only the parties would

00:43:21.920 --> 00:43:26.770
sort themselves out on ideological grounds,
we'd have a rational politics like Europe."

00:43:26.770 --> 00:43:29.569
Well we've done that and is everybody happy?

00:43:29.569 --> 00:43:31.140
Not exactly.

00:43:31.140 --> 00:43:38.910
When I came to Washington to work on the Republican
Senator's staff, 1970, the Senate was run

00:43:38.910 --> 00:43:47.020
by Eastland of Mississippi, Stennis of Mississippi,
Richard Russell from Winder, Georgia, McClellan

00:43:47.020 --> 00:43:49.809
from Arkansas, Talmadge from Georgia.

00:43:49.809 --> 00:43:52.069
It was entirely southern democrats.

00:43:52.069 --> 00:43:55.430
Not entirely, but close enough.

00:43:55.430 --> 00:43:58.059
There are no southern conservative democrats
anymore.

00:43:58.059 --> 00:43:59.671
There are no liberal republicans anymore.

00:43:59.671 --> 00:44:03.000
And is everybody happy?

00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:04.200
I don't think so.

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:06.450
Jonah: So, buy gold.

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:08.140
George: Yeah, okay.

00:44:08.140 --> 00:44:13.210
Jonah: Well, George...still very weird to
call you George, because you're Mr. Will in

00:44:13.210 --> 00:44:14.210
my eyes.

00:44:14.210 --> 00:44:15.940
But thank you very much for doing this.

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:16.940
Hey, everyone.

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:19.010
That's the end of our discussion with George
Will.

00:44:19.010 --> 00:44:20.410
Thanks for watching.

00:44:20.410 --> 00:44:24.680
If you enjoyed what you saw, remember to like
the video or leave us a comment, and be sure

00:44:24.680 --> 00:44:28.720
to check out the rest of our videos and research
from AEI.

